      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "handle-template-declaration",
          "name": "Handle Template",
          "version": "0.1.0",
          "description": "A Handle primitive: a reusable, addressable node (a View with payload 'handle.web') whose content becomes a custom decoration rendered ON a specific port -- alongside the default connection dot, not instead of it. The orb here is wired onto three of the Declaration's six ports, at three different angles, to show it rotating to face exactly outward every time."
        },
        "intent": {
          "kind": "template",
          "scope": "local"
        },
        "declaration": {
          "kind": "graph",
          "defaultSurfaceId": "web",
          "surfaces": []
        },
        "glyph": {
          "kind": "icon",
          "name": "Lens"
        },
        "name": "Declaration",
        "purpose": "",
        "document": {
          "url": "github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/handle-template/root.node"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "handle-template-view",
      "type": "view",
      "label": "View",
      "position": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 0
      },
      "width": 560,
      "height": 480,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "view": {
          "intent": "node",
          "payload": "node.web.detail"
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "handle-template",
          "nodeId": "handle-template-view"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "html",
          "value": "<div style='box-sizing:border-box;width:100%;height:100%;padding:28px;background:#ffffff;font-family:system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow:auto'><h1 style='margin:0 0 12px;font:800 24px/1.2 system-ui'>Handle</h1><p style='margin:0 0 12px;max-width:62ch;font:400 15px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>A reusable, addressable node -- a View with <code>data.view.payload: 'handle.web'</code> -- whose content (svg, html, or a canvas shape-spec) becomes a custom decoration drawn on a specific port, the same way a Glyph becomes what a Surface's chain ends in. It renders <strong>alongside</strong> the ordinary connection dot, not instead of it -- both are independent layers in HandleLayer.js, so nothing about a port's actual connection behavior changes by adding one.</p><h2 style='margin:20px 0 8px;font:700 16px/1.3 system-ui'>Wiring one on</h2><p style='margin:0 0 12px;max-width:62ch;font:400 15px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>A port's own <code>metadata.handlePresentation</code> names the Handle node directly: <code>{ sourceRef, sourceNodeId, sourcePayload: 'handle.web' }</code>. Three of this Declaration's six ports carry it -- <strong>surfaces</strong> (165&deg;), <strong>port</strong> (225&deg;), and <strong>landing surface</strong> (0&deg;) -- each showing the same orb at a different angle; the other three (default handle, boundary begin, boundary end) are left plain, proving both can sit on one node at once.</p><h2 style='margin:20px 0 8px;font:700 16px/1.3 system-ui'>Facing outward, precisely</h2><p style='margin:0 0 12px;max-width:62ch;font:400 15px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>HandleLayer.js rotates an authored decoration to <code>resolveHandleOutwardAngle</code>'s result -- normally just the nearest of four cardinal sides. This template also carries a small fix: computeHandlePosition already derives each handle's exact radial angle when it places it (<code>dx = cos(angle), dy = sin(angle)</code>); the presentation renderer now uses that exact angle when one was authored, instead of rounding down to a side. That's why the orb at 165&deg; doesn't just face 'left-ish' -- it faces exactly 165&deg;.</p><h2 style='margin:20px 0 8px;font:700 16px/1.3 system-ui'>On irregular shapes</h2><p style='margin:0;max-width:62ch;font:400 15px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>The outward-angle math that walks an actual polygon boundary and finds its true normal already exists and is already proven -- it's exactly what the <code>membrane-template</code> work exercises for a workspace tile's custom Membrane boundary. An ordinary node like this one is always a plain rectangle today, so that path isn't reachable from here; if nodes ever get their own custom outlines, the geometry to keep a Handle perpendicular to one is already written, just not yet wired to a per-node shape.</p></div>"
        }
      }
